Thanks a lot for the tip.  I went there, put on my oilskin cloak, killed 
the eels with magic missiles or bolts from Hellfire (unfortunately no 
means to freeze the moat), pulverized the statue, and got the scales. 
Zapped it with cancellation later, exchanged my red dragon scale mail 
(from the wyrm caves, kind of insurance against polymorph traps) against 
it, wrote a scroll of enchant armor, and inproved my AC even beyond its 
original value.

Since I have completed the lawful quest four or five times before, I 
find it strange that I did not notice the repeated occurrence of the 
scales.  Probably my attention was diminished by the fact that I did 
have magic resistance in all the cases before, and then a set of gray 
scales is not very interesting.  Also, in one case, I had to defeat the 
arch-lich, which can be very distracting, to say the least, and in 
another, I did not even bother to go there - just because of the arch-lich.

However, I find it very neat that there is a means to get magic 
resistance in an almost deterministic way, but that the armor is guarded 
by a trap that can result in an instadeath, if you don't have magic 
resistance...  Which brings up the question whether there is a way of 
safely getting rid of the arch-lich without having to genocide them? 
Often, you will not have a scroll of genocide before hitting the lawful 
quest and throne-genocides are even less reliable.  Trying to use 
striking or force bolt against the statue from afar does not work - it 
will simply come alive.

I noted in wizard mode that the arch-lich is somewhat slow in attacking 
you, so you probably always have one turn before he starts (if you are 
not burdened).  With free action, you could hit him with a potion of 
paralysis, and then shoot him with highly enchanted bullets. The 
question is then, how many potions of paralysis are necessary to make 
sure, he will not be able to move before being killed.  One move might 
be too many, because he might use the touch of death...  Of course, once 
he is paralysed, one can take a step away and read a (blessed or 
uncursed) scroll of earth.  This requires to have a means to kill him 
from the distance (MM spell, enchanted bullets, stream of acid spell). 
(Actually, once you are in your fort, you can cancel the dragon scales, 
put them on and then risk melee with the arch-lich.)
Alternatively, one could read the scroll immediately, and then use a 
wand of cause fear to make the arch-lich move away from his square, 
where he was hit by a boulder.  This is not hundred percent safe, as he 
might get in an attack.

Concerning my problem with Manlobbi, the black marketeer, it seems that 
one of my spells, drain level, works on him.  At least, he screams 
"Thief" every time I use it, and after three or four spells, when I used 
the last charge of my wand of probing, he was at level 25, which appears 
lowish for a black marketeer (normal shopkeepers are at 24 already), and 
what's more important, he was not at his max Hp.  Killing him this way 
will be slow, however, as it may require 20 or more spell castings, 
until his hexagonal amulet crumbles (nice way to identify life saving by 
probing "Sam"), and then about 28 more - since I cannot cast them all in 
a row, being limited by my mana points, he will have time to heal to the 
max Hp of his new level.  So I will just pay him a visit each time I 
pass Dlvl 22, and of course, I will try highly enchanted bullets on him 
as soon as I have enough.  He has a bag of holding in his shop (and so 
far, I have only a sack) that I filled with goodies when still 
contemplating to just steal it.  But my only dog pet was untamed by a 
koala and had left through the portal, so I can't get it back to 
Manlobbi.  Zombie pets that I summoned were much less useful, because 
instead of picking stuff up they unsuccessfully attacked Manlobbi.  Also 
I made the mistake of blessing the bag in order to make it easier for a 
pet to pick it up.  But undeads don't pick up blessed stuff and I had no 
means of creating monsters at the time to get a new cat or dog.
